package mop;

import javax.crypto.KeyGenerator;
import javax.crypto.SecretKey;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;

import br.unb.cic.mop.eh.*;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateStore;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateVerdict;
import br.unb.cic.mop.Property;

/**
 * SecretKeySpec
 *
 * Monitors javax.crypto.SecretKey against the CrySL rule SecretKey.crysl. The concrete class
 * javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec is monitored by a different specification of this set,
 * SecretKeySpecSpec.mop, which transcribes SecretKeySpec.crysl and checks the constructor.
 *
 * The rule's ORDER is `GetEnc*, Destroy?`. This specification declares only the `GetEnc`
 * event, `getEncoded()`. It states no CONSTRAINTS and no REQUIRES, and it ENSURES
 * `preparedKeyMaterial[keyMaterial] after GetEnc`, which the key-spec constructors of this set
 * read before they accept bytes as key material. Nothing here translates a constraint and
 * nothing here accuses: the specification has no row in `codes.csv`.
 *
 * `getEncoded()` returns a fresh copy on every call and the predicate store compares bound
 * objects by identity, so without this specification nothing established about a key would
 * ever reach a reader of the bytes it handed out. Bridging that copy is the whole of what this
 * file does, and it bridges it only for a key whose own origin the instrumentation observed --
 * a guard the rule does not state, which keeps hard-coded material from being laundered into
 * prepared material through a copy.
 *
 * Behaviour that departs from the rule: SecretKey.crysl also states
 * `NEGATES generatedKey[this, _] after Destroy`, and this specification never negates that
 * predicate, so a key destroyed after it was generated keeps its mark and the sites that
 * consume the mark go on accepting it. `destroy()` is not declared as an event, so the call is
 * invisible to the automaton rather than refused by it; it throws DestroyFailedException on
 * both SecretKey implementations this set can observe -- the SecretKeySpec class, and the key
 * KeyGenerator.generateKey() returns, which is that same class -- so an `after ... returning`
 * advice over it would have no execution path.
 *
 * @see https://github.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/blob/6d844ab402229aaefa4c5e45bf080987b787624b/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SecretKey.crysl
 */
SecretKeySpec(SecretKey secretKey) {

   // The bytes `getEncoded()` returned, staged from the event that observed them to the
   // handler that writes about them: a handler receives no event arguments and runs after the
   // transition is decided, so the array a call returned has to travel in a field.
   byte[] stagedKeyMaterial;

   // `GetEnc`: `ge1: keyMaterial = getEncoded();`, the whole of this specification. The rule
   // states no CONSTRAINTS and no REQUIRES, so this body checks no clause and reports nothing;
   // what it does is carry a predicate across a call that copies. `getEncoded()` returns a
   // fresh clone each time it is called, and the store is keyed on object identity, so no
   // reader of the returned bytes could otherwise see what was established about the material
   // they were copied from. This event is the only thing in the set that bridges that copy for
   // a secret key, which makes its read a propagation record rather than an accuser.
   //
   // The read asks for GENERATED_KEY over the key: the key was produced by a call the
   // instrumentation observed, for the algorithm recorded with it. It governs the staging and
   // not a report, so the three verdicts collapse into two here -- SATISFIED stages the array,
   // VIOLATED and NOT_OBSERVED stage nothing, and no site downstream can tell which of the two
   // it was. Staging only on SATISFIED is what refuses to launder key material through a copy:
   // a SecretKeySpec built from hard-coded bytes, reported at its own construction, would
   // otherwise hand its encoding on as prepared key material and a second SecretKeySpec built
   // from that encoding would be silent.
   //
   // The read sits in the event body and not in a `condition(...)` guard: a guard compiles to
   // an early return ahead of both the body and the transition, so a key whose origin the
   // monitor had not observed would leave the automaton instead of simply not propagating. Here
   // the transition is taken either way and the verdict decides one thing only, whether there
   // is anything to carry.
   //
   // The value asked with is the key's own algorithm, which is what each producer of
   // GENERATED_KEY writes for a secret key: `KeyGeneratorSpec` writes the name the program
   // handed `getInstance`, resolved through `ConscryptAliasTable`; `SecretKeySpecSpec` the name
   // its constructor was given; and `KeyStoreSpec` the algorithm of the key it returns. Those
   // spellings need not agree -- `KeyGenerator.getInstance("BLOWFISH")` returns a key that
   // reports `Blowfish` -- and what reconciles them is the store itself: a value position of
   // declared type String is compared case-insensitively, so the two spellings match.
   //
   // The owner carries the subtype operator, and it has to: `SecretKey` is an interface, and
   // the receiver a program actually holds is almost always a `SecretKeySpec`. Where the source
   // declares the variable as the concrete class, the compiler emits
   // `invoke-virtual Ljavax/crypto/spec/SecretKeySpec;->getEncoded()[B` -- a declared owner an
   // exact-match pointcut on the interface never sees, so without the `+` this event would not
   // weave for the receivers it exists for and the `preparedKeyMaterial` bridge below would
   // never fire for them. The matcher keeps the trailing `+` in the declaring type -- unlike a
   // parameter, where it is stripped to a flag -- and resolves the owner by walking the
   // ancestors of the receiver's class, interfaces included.
   //
   // The same copy would break a chain of reports in the middle, so the event bridges one more
   // fact: REPORTED_UPSTREAM, the object's producer already reported it, so a later site that
   // finds no predicate for it is looking at a refusal upstream and not at a gap in
   // instrumentation reach. A key that carries the mark hands it to the array it returns, and
   // `SecretKeySpecSpec` then reports a re-wrap of those bytes as refused upstream rather than
   // as not observed. The mark is not a CrySL predicate and has no acceptance point, so it is
   // written here in the body, where the returned array is visible.
   event e1 after(SecretKey secretKey) returning(byte[] key):
      call(public byte[] SecretKey+.getEncoded()) &&
      target(secretKey) {
        PredicateVerdict generated = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_KEY, secretKey,
            secretKey.getAlgorithm());
        if (generated == PredicateVerdict.SATISFIED) {
            stagedKeyMaterial = key;
        }
        if (PredicateStore.instance().validateAny(Property.REPORTED_UPSTREAM, secretKey) == PredicateVerdict.SATISFIED) {
            PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.REPORTED_UPSTREAM, key);
        }
   }

   // ENSURES `preparedKeyMaterial[keyMaterial] after GetEnc`: the bytes are key material a
   // monitored key or key agreement handed out.
   //
   // The clause carries `after GetEnc`, so it holds at the states `ge1` leads to; the `ere` is
   // `e1*`, whose only alias is `match`, over its accepting states. Here the two are the same
   // single state, so the handler below is the acceptance point under either reading, and no
   // `@fail` handler is written: `GetEnc*` accepts every sequence of the one event there is.
   //
   // The handler runs after every `e1`, which is what the clause needs: each call returns its
   // own copy and each copy has to be marked separately. The staged array is consumed here or
   // discarded, so a call whose key origin was not observed never leaves a marking behind for
   // the next one.
   ere : e1*

   @match {
     if (stagedKeyMaterial != null) {
         PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.PREPARED_KEY_MATERIAL, stagedKeyMaterial);
         stagedKeyMaterial = null;
     }
   }

}
